(10. REFLECTION ANSWERS \(<= 300 WORDS\)) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(Raw sockets require root privileges because they allow direct access to packet) Tj
0 -14 Td
(headers and low-level traffic that can bypass normal transport-layer safety) Tj
0 -14 Td
(controls. This access can be abused for spoofing, scanning, or packet) Tj
0 -14 Td
(manipulation, so the OS restricts it.) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(Raw sockets differ from normal TCP/UDP sockets because applications receive and) Tj
0 -14 Td
(interpret headers directly. In TCP/UDP sockets, the kernel handles most) Tj
0 -14 Td
(protocol logic \(connection state, reliability, segmentation/reassembly,) Tj
0 -14 Td
(checksums\) and applications read only payload-oriented streams/datagrams.) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(One advantage of raw sockets is full visibility/control of packet-level) Tj
0 -14 Td
(behavior, useful for protocol debugging and security tooling. One limitation is) Tj
0 -14 Td
(higher complexity and privilege requirements; implementation mistakes can easily) Tj
0 -14 Td
(cause incorrect parsing or unstable behavior.) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(One cybersecurity application is intrusion detection or custom packet inspection) Tj
0 -14 Td
(where tools analyze live header/flag patterns \(for example, SYN flood patterns) Tj
0 -14 Td
(or anomalous protocol usage\).) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(11. CONCLUSION) Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
() Tj
0 -14 Td
(The TCP raw socket implementation successfully captured and decoded 20 packets) Tj
0 -14 Td
(with required IP and TCP fields. Generated traffic was verified against) Tj
